---
import BrandArtwork from "../../components/BrandArtwork.astro";
import Layout from "../../layouts/ResourceLayout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="What is a harness? — Modesto"
  description="How to structure your codebase for AI agents. Why pure functions, clear boundaries, and determinism matter more than ever."
>
  <article class="article-shell">

    <header class="article-header">
      <div class="article-eyebrow">
        <a href="/articles" class="back mono">← Blog</a>
        <div style="display: flex; gap: 16px; align-items: center;">
          <button type="button" id="download-pdf-btn" onclick="window.print()" class="mono" style="background: none; border: none; color: var(--faint); font-size: 9px; letter-spacing: 0.08em; cursor: pointer; transition: color 150ms ease;">Download PDF ↓</button>
          <span class="mono article-date">August 17, 2026</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="article-cover"><BrandArtwork /></div>
      <div class="article-header-copy">
        <span class="mono article-cat">Engineering</span>
        <h1>What is a<br />harness?</h1>
        <p class="article-lede">
          If you want an AI agent to write code for you, you need to give it a place to work. A codebase that relies on hidden state, implicit dependencies, and manual setup will cause an agent to thrash. You need a harness.
        </p>
        <div class="article-meta">
          <span class="mono">Modesto Team</span>
          <span class="meta-sep mono">·</span>
          <span class="mono">8 min read</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="article-rule"></div>

    <div class="article-body">

      <p>
        When a human engineer joins your team, they spend the first two weeks learning how to build the project, how to run the tests, and which undocumented environment variables are strictly required to make the local database connect. They learn the unwritten rules of your architecture.
      </p>

      <p>
        An AI coding agent does not have two weeks. It has the context window you give it right now.
      </p>

      <p>
        If your codebase requires tribal knowledge to navigate—if functions have side effects that only make sense if you know about a specific row in a specific database table, or if the UI components are tightly coupled to a global state machine that is mutated implicitly—an agent will struggle. It will hallucinate imports. It will write tests that pass but break the build. It will thrash.
      </p>

      <h2>The definition of a harness</h2>

      <p>
        A <strong>harness</strong> is an architectural pattern designed to isolate complexity. In the context of AI-assisted development, a harness is a clear, deterministic boundary that you place around the code you want the agent to write.
      </p>

      <p>
        Think of it like a test harness in traditional software engineering. A test harness provides the inputs, execution environment, and assertions necessary to verify that a module works. An agent harness provides the same isolation, but for <em>writing</em> the code in the first place.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        An agent harness is a promise: "If you implement this interface, with these inputs, and return these outputs, the rest of the system will work."
      </blockquote>

      <h2>Why harnesses matter for agents</h2>

      <p>
        Agents like Codex, Claude, and Gemini are extraordinarily good at translating clear requirements into code. They are less good at discovering implicit dependencies across a massive, unstructured monolith.
      </p>

      <p>
        When you use a harness, you drastically reduce the context window required for the agent to succeed. You aren't asking the agent to "fix the billing bug in the monolith." You are asking the agent to "implement this `calculateProratedRefund` function, which takes a `Subscription` object and a `Date` and returns a `Currency` amount."
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Determinism:</strong> The agent can run unit tests locally and know immediately if its solution is correct, without needing to spin up a docker-compose cluster.</li>
        <li><strong>Isolation:</strong> The agent cannot accidentally break the payment gateway integration because the interface it is coding against has no access to the network.</li>
        <li><strong>Confidence:</strong> You, the human reviewer, can review the diff quickly. You don't have to trace a sprawling set of changes across 15 files; you just review the pure function implementation.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Designing for the AI era</h2>

      <p>
        We talk a lot at Modesto about unified workspaces and checkpoint handoffs. But the truth is, the tools can only do so much if the underlying code resists being understood.
      </p>

      <p>
        Structuring your code with clear boundaries, pure functions, and explicit dependencies isn't just "good engineering practice" anymore. It is the prerequisite for letting autonomous systems help you build. The teams that will move fastest in the next five years are the ones who learn how to build the best harnesses.
      </p>

    </div>

    <div class="article-rule"></div>

    <footer class="article-footer">
      <div class="article-footer-left">
        <span class="mono">Modesto · August 2026</span>
        <p>Modesto is available now for macOS, Windows, and Linux. Local-first, model-agnostic, built for how software teams actually work with AI agents.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="article-footer-right">
        <a href="/articles" class="mono back-link">← All articles</a>
      </div>
    </footer>

  </article>

  <!-- Print-only branded footer -->
  <div class="article-print-footer" aria-hidden="true">
    <img src="/modesto-logo.svg" alt="" />
    <span>Modesto</span>
    <span class="article-print-footer-url">modesto.dev/articles</span>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .article-print-footer {
    display: none;
  }

  .article-shell {
    width: min(100% - 48px, 1440px);
    margin: 0 auto;
    border-inline: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  /* ── Header ── */
  .article-header {
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .article-eyebrow {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    padding: 20px clamp(28px, 8vw, 120px);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .back {
    color: var(--faint);
    font-size: 9px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: color 150ms ease;
  }

  .back:hover {
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .article-date {
    color: var(--faint);
    font-size: 9px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
  }

  .article-cover {
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 6;
    overflow: hidden;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .article-cover img {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    display: block;
  }

  .article-header-copy {
    padding: clamp(40px, 7vw, 80px) clamp(28px, 8vw, 120px);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 20px;
    background: radial-gradient(ellipse 60% 45% at 50% -10%, color-mix(in srgb, var(--violet) 10%, transparent), transparent 68%);
  }

  .article-cat {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 9px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
  }

  .article-header h1 {
    font-size: clamp(3rem, 7vw, 7rem);
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: -0.07em;
    line-height: 0.88;
    color: var(--ink);
    max-width: 900px;
  }

  .article-lede {
    max-width: 600px;
    font-size: clamp(15px, 1.6vw, 18px);
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--muted);
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  }

  .article-meta {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    color: var(--faint);
    font-size: 9px;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
  }

  .meta-sep {
    opacity: 0.5;
  }

  /* ── Rule ── */
  .article-rule {
    height: 1px;
    background: var(--line);
  }

  /* ── Body ── */
  .article-body {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr min(680px, 100%) 1fr;
    padding: clamp(60px, 8vw, 100px) 0;
    row-gap: 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .article-body > * {
    grid-column: 2;
  }

  .article-body p {
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.78;
    color: var(--ink);
    margin-bottom: 28px;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  }

  .article-body h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 3vw, 2.6rem);
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: -0.05em;
    line-height: 1.1;
    color: var(--ink);
    margin-top: 64px;
    margin-bottom: 28px;
  }

  .article-body ul {
    margin: 0 0 28px 0;
    padding-left: 24px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 10px;
  }

  .article-body li {
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--ink);
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  }

  .article-body blockquote {
    margin: 48px 0;
    padding: 32px 36px;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--accent);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--paper-deep) 60%, transparent);
    font-size: clamp(1.15rem, 2vw, 1.5rem);
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    line-height: 1.4;
    color: var(--ink);
    font-style: normal;
  }

  /* ── Footer ── */
  .article-footer {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr auto;
    gap: 40px;
    align-items: end;
    padding: clamp(48px, 6vw, 80px) clamp(28px, 8vw, 120px);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .article-footer-left {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 16px;
  }

  .article-footer-left > .mono {
    color: var(--faint);
    font-size: 9px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
  }

  .article-footer-left p {
    max-width: 480px;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  .article-cta {
    display: inline-flex;
    gap: 24px;
    padding-bottom: 6px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--ink);
    text-decoration: none;
    width: fit-content;
    transition: color 150ms ease;
  }

  .article-cta:hover {
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .back-link {
    color: var(--faint);
    font-size: 9px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: color 150ms ease;
  }

  .back-link:hover {
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .article-shell {
      width: min(100% - 32px, 1440px);
    }

    .article-body {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      padding-inline: clamp(20px, 5vw, 40px);
    }

    .article-body > * {
      grid-column: 1;
    }

    .article-footer {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
  /* ── Print / "Download PDF" ──
     No PDF library — reuses the browser's own print-to-PDF against the page
     content already rendered. Keeps the real theme colors/gradients instead
     of flattening to plain black-on-white, hides site chrome (nav, the ←
     Blog/Download PDF/date bar, this article's own closing footer, and the
     global site footer) so only the cover image through the end of the
     article prints, and shows a small branded footer in their place. */
  @media print {
    * {
      -webkit-print-color-adjust: exact;
      print-color-adjust: exact;
      color-adjust: exact;
    }

    .site-header,
    .global-footer,
    .footer-mark,
    .capability-ticker,
    .article-eyebrow,
    .article-footer {
      display: none !important;
    }

    body {
      background: #ffffff;
      color: #14151a;
    }

    .article-shell {
      width: 100%;
      border-inline: 0;
    }

    /* Safari's print engine is well documented to mis-render CSS Grid (and
       occasionally flex) during the print pass — tracks can collapse or
       children can lose their placement, which is what "ignores all our
       styling" looks like from the outside even though the colors/fonts
       below are applied correctly. Every layout still visible in print gets
       forced to plain block flow here so there's no grid/flex left for
       Safari to get wrong; Chrome/Firefox render identically either way. */
    body,
    .article-body,
    .article-body p,
    .article-body li,
    .article-header,
    .article-header-copy {
      font-family: "DM Sans", -apple-system, "Helvetica Neue", sans-serif;
    }

    .mono,
    .article-meta,
    .article-print-footer {
      font-family: "DM Mono", ui-monospace, monospace;
    }

    .article-header {
      display: block;
      border-bottom: 0;
    }

    .article-header-copy {
      display: block;
    }

    .article-cover,
    .article-header-copy {
      break-inside: avoid;
      page-break-inside: avoid;
    }

    .article-header h1 {
      color: #14151a;
    }

    .article-lede,
    .article-meta {
      color: #4a4d57;
    }

    .article-body {
      display: block;
      width: min(680px, 100%);
      margin: 0 auto;
      padding-bottom: 0;
      border-bottom: 0;
    }

    .article-body > * {
      grid-column: unset;
    }

    .article-body p {
      color: #2b2d33;
      orphans: 3;
      widows: 3;
    }

    .article-body h2 {
      color: #14151a;
      break-after: avoid;
      page-break-after: avoid;
      break-inside: avoid;
      page-break-inside: avoid;
    }

    .article-body li {
      color: #2b2d33;
    }

    .article-body blockquote,
    .article-body ul,
    .article-body img {
      break-inside: avoid;
      page-break-inside: avoid;
    }

    .article-body blockquote {
      background: #f4f4f6;
      color: #14151a;
    }

    .article-body code {
      background: #f0f0f2;
      border-color: #ddd;
      color: #14151a;
    }

    /* Normal document flow, not position:fixed — a fixed-position footer
       gets painted at the same screen coordinate on every printed page and
       can overlap the last line or two of that page's content instead of
       repeating cleanly, which is what was making text unreadable near page
       breaks. This renders once, after the article ends. */
    .article-print-footer {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 8px;
      height: 34px;
      margin-top: 32px;
      padding: 0 4px;
      border-top: 1px solid #ddd;
      color: #6b6e78;
      font: 500 10px "DM Sans", sans-serif;
    }

    .article-print-footer img {
      width: 14px;
      height: 14px;
      object-fit: contain;
    }

    .article-print-footer span:first-of-type {
      color: #14151a;
      font-weight: 600;
    }

    .article-print-footer-url {
      margin-left: auto;
    }
  }
</style>
